WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.740
The pioneers of wireless saw it as a gift to all the people.
无线电广播的先驱们把它视为送给全体民众的礼物。

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:18.990
Sir John Reith said that it would end “isolation of the spirit” and rejoiced: “It does not matter how many thousands may be listening; there is always enough for others…the genius and the fool, the wealthy and the poor listen simultaneously.”
约翰·里斯爵士说，无线电广播将终结“精神上的孤立”，并欣喜地表示：“不管可能有几千几万人在收听，都没有关系；总会有足够的份留给别人……天才和傻瓜、富人和穷人同时在听。”

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:25.390
Between two great wars this technological innovation built a new kind of national consciousness.
在两次大战之间的那些年里，这项技术创新塑造了一种新型的国民意识。

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:37.440
Opening this week, a book and exhibition curated by Beaty Rubens at the Bodleian in Oxford records how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939.
本周，由比蒂·鲁本斯策划的一本书和一场展览在牛津博德利图书馆推出，记录了1922年至1939年间广播如何改变日常生活。

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:46.230
She draws on letters, diaries and fiction, and a 1939 field notebook of verbatim audience research by Winifred Gill.
她取材于书信、日记和小说，以及威妮弗雷德·吉尔1939年的一本实地调查笔记，里面是逐字记下的听众调查。

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.370
There’s fun in testimonies of people enjoying the sheer newness.
人们讲述自己如何享受这份十足新鲜感的证言，读来饶有趣味。

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:55.350
A cartoon mocks a group failing to converse because they’re all in headphones.
一幅漫画嘲讽一群人因为都戴着耳机而无法交谈。

00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.150
People report that broadcast music made workmen whistle new tunes.
据人们讲述，广播播放的音乐让工人们用口哨吹起了新曲子。

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:08.150
A woman says there have been fewer street fights since the arrival of the wireless but also less stopping and “talking on the brush handle”.
一位女士说，自从收音机出现，街头斗殴少了，但人们停下脚步、“拄着扫帚把聊天”的时候也少了。

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.070
By and large the wireless was welcome.
总的来说，收音机是受人欢迎的。

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:21.530
I loved the man from the Thirties research who found that wireless suddenly offered “a lot of variety…things I thought I’d never be interested in…ice hockey perhaps”.
20世纪30年代那项调查里有一位男士，我很喜欢他：他发现收音机一下子提供了“种类繁多的东西……那些我原以为自己永远不会感兴趣的东西……也许是冰球吧”。

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:32.180
True: for more than 80 pre-digital years, linear speech broadcasting brought the gift of serendipity, random enlivening of a car journey or dull manual task.
的确如此：在数字时代到来之前的80多年里，线性播出的语言广播带来了意外之喜这份礼物，让一次驾车出行或一件枯燥的体力活偶然间变得生动有趣起来。

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:46.470
In my own book about radio I recorded how, on one drive, “I caught up with the news, learnt some 17th-century history, and was startlingly educated by an unpretentious programme on the history of the stethoscope.”
在我自己写的关于广播的书里，我记录了有一次开车途中的经历：“我了解了最新新闻，学了些17世纪的历史，还从一档朴实无华、讲听诊器历史的节目里长了见识，收获大得惊人。”

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:49.830
But radio’s enriching serendipity is ebbing.
但广播那种让生活更丰富的意外之喜正在消退。

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:56.210
With multiple networks and countless podcasts, a smartphone user selects what to hear and when.
有了众多广播网和数不清的播客，智能手机用户自己选择听什么、什么时候听。

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:06.120
And while it is wonderful to take a walk with anything in your headphones, infinite choice encourages us to shrink into niche interests and sympathetic beliefs.
而且，虽然散步时耳机里想听什么就听什么，这很美妙，但无限的选择会促使我们缩进小众兴趣和合自己心意的观点之中。

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.780
A hundred years on from Marconi and Reith, is the art of mere listening endangered?
从马可尼和里斯那时算起，一百年过去了，单纯聆听这门艺术是否已经濒危？

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:18.500
Some will say the audiobook boom revives it, though I suppose you can then worry about the decline of reading.
有人会说，有声书的热潮让这门艺术重获新生，不过我想，这样一来，你又可以担心阅读的衰落了。

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:28.490
But inventions shape all of us and it is worth noticing when techno-social habits do change, and asking whether to control them a bit or shield the youngest.
但发明塑造着我们每一个人；当与技术相关的社会习惯确实发生变化时，值得加以留意，并问一问：是否要对这些习惯稍加控制，或者保护好年纪最小的人。

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.100
Whatever we do, innovation will happen.
无论我们怎么做，创新总会发生。

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:47.400
Today we fret about the isolating culture of smartphone-staring and selfie-vanity, but already in 1939 there was that lady regretting how, when all her street got wirelesses, it lost the neighbourly habit of “talking on the brush handle”.
如今，我们为盯着智能手机、靠自拍满足虚荣心这种使人孤立的文化而发愁，但早在1939年，就有那位女士惋惜地说起，等到她那条街家家户户都有了收音机，整条街便失去了“拄着扫帚把聊天”这一邻里习惯。

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.960
It’s enough to make a person put down the smartphone and go out front with a yard broom.
这足以让人放下智能手机，拿上一把扫院子用的扫帚走到门前去。
